## Why This Is Asked
Tests involving real-time notifications with WebSockets and a message broker are common in production systems. This question evaluates practical measurement skills rather than theoretical definitions.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket concurrency management
- End-to-end latency and tail latency (p95/p99)
- Backpressure and buffering strategies
- Throughput vs. latency tradeoffs
- Observability across brokers and worker pools

## Code Example
```javascript
// WebSocket load test using k6
import ws from 'k6/ws';
import { check } from 'k6';

export const options = {
  vus: 100000,
  duration: '5m',
  thresholds: {
    'ws_latency_p95': ['p(95)<500'],
    'ws_latency_p99': ['p(99)<1000'],
  },
};

export default function () {
  const url = 'wss://notification-service/ws';
  const res = ws.connect(url, {}, function (socket) {
    socket.on('message', (data) => {
      socket.markPing();
    });
    socket.on('close', () => {});
    socket.setTimeout(() => {
      socket.close();
    }, 30000);
  });
  check(res, { 'status is 101': (r) => r && r.status === 101 });
}
```

## Metrics to Capture
| Metric | Target | Warning |
|--------|--------|--------|
| End-to-end latency p95 | <500ms | >500ms |
| End-to-end latency p99 | <1s | >1s |
| Kafka consumer lag | <1000 | >1000 |
| WebSocket reconnect rate | <1% | >1% |
| Worker pool utilization | <80% | >80% |

## Workload Model
- **Steady state**: 50k concurrent connections, 10 events/sec
- **Burst**: Scale to 100k connections, 1000 events/sec over 30 seconds
- **Cool-down**: Return to steady state over 60 seconds

## Failure Scenarios
1. **Kafka broker failure**: Restart broker, measure consumer group rebalance time
2. **Worker pool exhaustion**: Artificially limit pool size, observe backpressure
3. **Network partition**: Disconnect 20% of clients, verify graceful reconnection

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design comprehensive performance testing for a complex, multi-component real-time system. It tests understanding of end-to-end latency measurement across distributed components, resilience testing with external dependencies, and validation of autoscaling behavior under variable load conditions.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket connection scalability and lifecycle management
- Kafka throughput optimization, topic partitioning strategies, and consumer lag monitoring
- Redis data access patterns for presence tracking and rate limiting implementations
- Backpressure mechanisms and circuit breaker patterns for fault tolerance
- Autoscaling trigger configuration and failover strategies for external gateway dependencies

## Code Example
```javascript
import ws from 'k6/ws';
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check } from 'k6';

export const options = {
  stages: [
    { duration: '2m', target: 30000 },
    { duration: '5m', target: 60000 },
    { duration: '2m', target: 100000 },
  ],
};

export default function () {
  const url = __ENV.WS_URL;
  const response = ws.connect(url, {}, function (socket) {
    socket.on('open', () => {
      console.log('Connected');
    });

    socket.on('message', (message) => {
      const latency = Date.now() - JSON.parse(message).timestamp;
      check(latency, {
        'latency < 120ms': latency < 120,
      });
    });
  });

  check(response, { 'status is 101': (r) => r && r.status === 101 });
}
```

## Test Execution Strategy
Implement gradual load ramping to validate autoscaling triggers, inject fault scenarios to test circuit breaker functionality, and simulate external gateway outages to verify failover mechanisms. Monitor system-wide metrics to ensure performance targets are met under various conditions.

## Why This Is Asked

